WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:02.000
- [Stephen] Happy to be talking to

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.057
Professor Matthew Frye
Jacobson about his recent book,

00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:06.010
"One Grain of Sand" that was published

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.350
by Bloomsbury Academic Press in 2019.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.060
Professor Jacobson, thanks
for being here with us today.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.510
- Thank you for having me.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.660
I'm delighted to be talking with you.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:17.900
- [Stephen] So it was
exciting to read your book

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.660
about Odetta and in particular

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.970
her album "One Grain of Sand."

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.330
And I'd love to hear you talk first

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.040
a little bit about
Odetta's life and career

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.290
and about the family
that she was born into.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.290
- Okay, Odetta was born
in depression-era Alabama,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.640
but left for the West Coast
at a fairly young age.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.330
She was four or five when her family left.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.220
And in that sense, she's very much a child

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.880
of the great migration,
in that she did get

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.350
at least a short experience
of the Jim Crow South.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:58.290
And in fact, some of her earliest memories

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.200
of overt racism were actually on the train

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.203
on the way out of Alabama.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.250
But they settled in Los
Angeles where she grew up

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.450
very much in a kind of more cosmopolitan

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:16.450
certainly than Alabama, in the sense that

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.510
one of her best childhood friends

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:23.510
was a Japanese American girl
who ended up being interned

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:28.550
shortly after Odetta's
family moved to Los Angeles.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.300
So it was a more mixed and
cosmopolitan kind of setting

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.760
than the one she had been born into.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.760
But largely an African American culture

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.630
and largely transplanted,
and recently transplanted

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.090
from the South.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:53.090
Her mother was what in the
day would have been called

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.050
probably a washer woman or
perhaps a cleaning woman,

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.420
but she worked for a theater troupe.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.090
And in fact, that ended up
being Odetta's first connection

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.490
to the theater.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.490
It was that community
that recognized in Odetta,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.080
as a fairly young girl, the
kind of talent that she had

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.203
and that she might be able to hone.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.290
And that community actually pitched in

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.680
to sponsor her classical training.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.680
So she was classically trained as a singer

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.360
and ultimately a performer.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.360
She goes on to attend
college in the theater arts

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.300
and really is studying
to be a classical singer.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.453
People talked about her as
the next Marian Anderson,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.440
which ultimately is something
that she chaffed at.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.160
She says, "I didn't wanna
be the next anything.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.350
I wanted to be me."

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:52.780
And she found her way kind of flukishly

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.780
into the folk world in
North Beach and the Bay Area

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.580
and decided to throw aside everything

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.980
that she had been trained to do

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.703
and to pick up folk music,
that was in the early '50s.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.600
She'd been in a theater production.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.890
And probably there's no question,
she had a future in that

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.143
if she had chosen to stay with it.

00:03:19.143 --> 00:03:22.080
She was in "Finian's Rainbow" in 1949.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.710
That was the direction
she was headed into,

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.680
but she really chucked it
aside in the early '50s.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.323
And by 1954, she's
cutting her first album,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.650
which is a folk album.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.510
And I think that is the
thing that drew me to her

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.720
as an object of study.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.190
And as, I mean, just someone
who I admire so much.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:48.940
The beauty of her music
and the power of her voice

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.903
and her talent is just, it's majestic.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.190
But I was really interested
in that story of,

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.293
how does a young African American woman,

00:04:02.771 --> 00:04:07.420
around 20, 21, decide
that she wants to make

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.590
this kind of unheard of jump
from the classical world

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:16.250
that she's really been
trained for and reared for,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.200
into the uncertain world of folk music.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.750
And I just think it's a fascinating story

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:27.060
from just the kind of
performance and arts angle,

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.270
but the politics of it
are also fascinating.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:33.290
I mean, she from the
beginning really talked about

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:38.290
the folk repertoire as a
resource for African Americans.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.700
So her first album is 1954.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:48.170
She's already fairly famous
nationally by 1957, 1958.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.740
This is a period where there
really was no such thing

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.290
as African American studies in
the curriculum, at any level.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:00.690
And she recognized the
repertoire by which I mean,

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.703
the field hollers, the
prison songs, the blues,

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.010
as a resource for African Americans

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.740
to recover their own history

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.250
and to know something about,
not only where they had been,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.280
but also to know something
of their resilience

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.550
and their inner strength and power.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.510
And that's how she talked
about the repertoire.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:23.930
And she always talked about
herself as both an archivist

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.530
and an educator in that sense.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.363
Other people talked about
her that way too, ultimately.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:32.340
But that was another piece of it

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.020
that was just really fascinating to me.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.370
And that really underscored
what an important figure

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.313
she was in her moment.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.660
- [Stephen] Why don't you take
us to "One Grain of Sand."

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.730
Tell us a little bit about the album

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.690
and why you decided to write
about "One Grain of Sand."

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.670
- So this book was written
really on assignment

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.150
for a series that's
called "33 and a Third."

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:00.563
And the assignment is to pick
an album and write about it.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.970
I wanted to write about her.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.050
I should say, I mean, this project spun

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.570
out of another project.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.290
There was going to be an Odetta chapter

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.870
in a much broader book
about the cultural history

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.113
of the civil rights era.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.263
So she had been on my mind for some time.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.410
When the opportunity to write
for this series came up,

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.470
I listened to all of her
albums and really thought about

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:31.063
where I wanted to enter this conversation.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.290
Musically, there might've been
different choices to make.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:39.030
I think most people might
have chosen her 1957 album

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.780
as the one that's maybe that
was kind of her signature.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.750
But "One Grain of Sand"
is by anybody's measure

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.080
one of her best albums and
one of her most important.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.430
And for me as a historian,

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.850
the most important thing about it was

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:56.850
that it was released in 1963,
the same year that she appears

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:02.050
on the stage alongside all of
those other luminous figures

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.883
in both the arts and politics
at the March on Washington.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.260
But it's also, this was
the occasion of the march,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.500
it was the 100-year anniversary

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.890
of the Emancipation Proclamation.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:19.890
And so as a historian kind
of entering this work,

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.800
thinking about it as a
historian's meditation

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.800
on an artistic production,
that was really compelling

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.300
because her repertoire and
her approach to the repertoire

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.360
and the moment of the album's
release gave me permission

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:42.880
to reflect on that 100 years
of African American history

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.170
between the Emancipation Proclamation

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:48.490
and the March on Washington.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:52.040
And in a sense, that's how
I would describe the book.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.980
It's a historian's
meditation on that 100 years

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:59.160
through the lens of Odetta's own artistic

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.593
and archival practices.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.110
- [Stephen] And most immediately,

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.950
in advance of the release of the album

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.240
on January 1st, 1963,
what was Odetta doing?

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.960
What was happening in the United States

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.920
that perhaps shaped the
production of "One Grain of Sand"

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.907
in advance of its release
on January 1st, 1963?

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:21.860
- Well, she had been traveling
through the folk world

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.833
almost for 10 years at that point,

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.593
just about 10 years at that point.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:36.910
And had been a fairly
well known national figure

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.043
in those circles for the
last five of those years.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.580
This is a moment when to be a folk singer

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.000
really was to be something.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.650
There was a whole circuit of
really important folk venues

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:57.130
from the West Coast through
cities like Denver and Chicago,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.870
to the East Coast, especially in New York,

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.043
but also Philadelphia and Boston.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:07.760
And she was really one of
the mainstays on that circuit

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.073
in the late '50s.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.830
It was in those years that she was

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.470
not only honing her
craft as a folk singer,

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.010
but expanding her repertoire.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:22.990
She spent a good deal of
time actually studying

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:27.990
the old Smithsonian Lomax
recordings in the libraries

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:31.050
of the University of California

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.820
when she was still living there.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.680
She spent a lot of time really
as a student of the music

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:42.440
and recognizing the political
import of the movement,

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.188
of the music.

00:09:44.188 --> 00:09:46.950
She never fancied herself

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.160
a terribly important political figure,

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.160
even if she totally felt that
there was political import

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.560
to what she was trying
to do as a musician.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.580
So when she showed up at
the March on Washington,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:06.960
for example, it was at the
invitation of other people.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.790
There were other people who
said, you gotta be there.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:09.940
We have to have you there.

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:13.900
We need Odetta's voice as part of this.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.480
And she always answered that call.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.960
So, that kind of thing had
probably started happening

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.830
a few years earlier.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:22.530
So she was already well known

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:24.944
on the political circuits at that point,

00:10:24.944 --> 00:10:28.630
but much more well known
just as a folk singer

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:31.890
who had a really deep and
rich kind of repertoire

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.420
that reflected something important

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.320
about the African American experience.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:38.727
- [Stephen] Well, it's a
beautifully structured book.

00:10:38.727 --> 00:10:43.500
And you talk about the ways
in which "One Grain of Sand"

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:47.210
embodies Odetta's approach
to the folk repertoire.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:48.890
It says something about how she worked

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:50.540
with the archive of black history

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:54.380
and was also for her a vehicle
for radical expression,

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:55.430
in your words.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:56.943
Can you say a little bit more

00:10:56.943 --> 00:11:00.640
about how you organized
the book into sections

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.840
and how you decided which
songs on "One Grain of Sand"

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.113
to look closely at?

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.730
- Sure, yeah.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.730
Well, I will say there are
two pieces of it that were

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:16.733
the germs or the kernels
of the whole idea.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:20.670
One was something I understood very well

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:24.180
and the other was something I
felt I needed to understand,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.460
but didn't quite.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.500
The thing I understood very well

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:31.880
was the significance of that
folk coffee house circuit,

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.853
that I just described to you.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:39.180
As a political and social
space, I just thought that

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.390
these venues represented a
really important crossroads

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:46.950
in the culture where all kinds of people

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.490
were coming together in an era

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.583
when the left really needed that.

00:11:52.578 --> 00:11:54.340
I think one of the things
that we often forget

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.590
about the modern civil rights era

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:00.760
is that the civil rights
public that had existed

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.760
before the war was just
decimated by McCarthyism

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.083
and by the kind of postwar,

00:12:09.404 --> 00:12:11.833
cold war politics of anti-communism.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:17.870
And so if there was going to be a revival

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.260
of a real robust kind of
civil rights politics,

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.170
as we know there was, the first thing

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.432
that was gonna have to happen

00:12:27.432 --> 00:12:29.500
was a new civil rights public

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.860
was gonna have to be
kind of found and forged

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:34.723
and encouraged and nourished.

00:12:36.375 --> 00:12:39.060
This is in some ways, it's
a story that's been told

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:42.800
as the transition from the
old left to the new left.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.520
It's a story that's been told
in terms of the transition

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.410
from the black church to secular spaces,

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:53.740
like college campuses,
all of that is important.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.810
But I think that these coffee
houses in the late '50s

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:01.693
and early '60s were a place
where old left folk singers

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.020
like Pete Seeger and The Weavers

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:10.333
and new left figures like
Joan Baez and Bob Dylan,

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:16.260
old kind of blues figures
and their publics,

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:20.870
like Huddie Ledbetter and
the kind of Alan Lomax crowd,

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:25.753
new folk singers like
Odetta herself, beat poets,

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.760
it was just a really protean space

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.630
where all kinds of people came together

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.490
and you could see at some of these clubs,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.500
like the Gate of Horn in Chicago

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:37.860
or the hungry i in San Francisco.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:41.850
You might hear Odetta singing
field hollers one night

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:46.220
and then hear a kind of left comedian,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.900
like Mort Sahl the next night

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:52.440
and then hear a blacklisted
folk singer like Will Geer

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.580
the night after that.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.840
You just didn't know kind
of what you were gonna see

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.260
from night-to-night or week-to-week.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:59.110
And I think that the publics

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:00.710
that crossed through those spaces

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:05.710
are really important to the
story of the early civil rights,

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.990
the postwar iteration of the
civil rights, freedom struggle.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:15.480
So that was a piece that
I knew I wanted to tell.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.090
And the song that I chose to do that with

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:21.910
was "Cool Water," which
was a kind of folk standard

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:23.770
initially sung as a cowboy song.

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.810
Made most famous probably by Roy Rogers,

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.710
although he wasn't the first to do it.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:31.020
But Odetta's version is just,

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:34.130
what she does musically with
it is completely different.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:36.090
It was to serve a very different purpose.

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.573
She sung it in a metaphorical register.

00:14:40.219 --> 00:14:41.760
It's quite literally about a cowboy

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.290
and his horse dying of
thirst in the desert.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:48.313
And she turns it into
this kind of metaphorical,

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:54.750
really powerful kind of allegory

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:59.750
about the cool water of
freedom and being denied that.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.590
So I used the occasion of that song

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.380
and what she does generically

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:09.960
and artistically with
that song to think about

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.510
the political work that was being done

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:14.490
in those coffee house spaces,

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.623
by the cultural work that
singers like her were doing.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.810
So that was kind of in place
before I started writing.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:23.530
That was something I knew I wanted to do.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:26.750
The piece that I knew
I needed to deal with,

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:28.260
but I didn't know what to do with it,

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:32.800
was the song "Midnight Special,"

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:35.040
which is probably the most
famous song on the album.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.020
It's a song that it's
been done so many times

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.420
by so many different people,

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:42.940
it's in some ways the most
familiar of all the songs

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.530
that's on that album.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:48.220
I was always a little bit confused by it.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:52.070
It's a song, the Midnight
Special is a train,

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:55.890
and the refrain is shine your light me.

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.500
And I never could figure out,

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.590
the lyrics are clearly,
it's about a prison house,

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:03.970
so there's that dimension.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:05.560
It's a little bit hard to follow.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:09.320
And I was never sure whether
shine your light on me meant

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:13.100
that the train was going to
redeem the narrator of the song

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.610
and take him or her away,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:19.140
or whether it was actually a suicide song.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:21.280
And in researching the song,

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.200
which dates back to the late 19th century

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:26.400
and various versions,
what I discovered is that

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.970
you can find versions that emphasize one

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.090
or the other of those things.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:33.380
Odetta herself actually
really played with that.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:36.073
So her versions changed over the time.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:40.650
So that in the 1957 version that she cut,

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.300
it's very clearly the song of redemption.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:47.918
Whereas in the 1963 version,
that's on "One Grain of Sand"

00:16:47.918 --> 00:16:50.160
she's emphasized different things,

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.870
she's reordered the verses in such a way

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:56.583
that it's clearly a song
about death on the tracks.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:01.850
So that was a puzzle that
I was trying to figure out.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:03.870
Figuring out how to understand the song,

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:07.560
but also placing it in the long kind of,

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.190
long live genre of the prison song,

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:12.350
which is so important in the
African American tradition.

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:17.350
And so, again, it was a piece
of a pretty long stretch

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:22.710
of African American history
that could be meditated upon

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:26.780
and told through the
history of the song itself

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:30.180
and its various variations over the years,

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.790
including Odetta's own
variations on the theme.

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:35.990
So those were the two
pieces I started with.

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:38.450
And then the two pieces I added,

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:43.450
one is on the spiritual
kind of strain of the music

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:49.240
and the other is on, there
are two Southern songs,

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:51.170
one is about the boll weevil,

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:55.623
and the other is about
the old cotton plantation.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.490
So taking kind of spiritual geographies

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:02.830
and social geographies and
again talking about those themes

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:05.410
through songs on the album.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.310
That became the strategy for the album

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:09.660
was to try to think really
deeply about history,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:14.660
but to do it through the
kinds of questions and answers

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:18.540
that were at hand in the music itself.

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:20.680
Which is for me, that's one of the things

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.410
that was such a delight about
working on this project.

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:25.980
There was a lot of freedom in terms of

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.263
what I could do in this assignment,

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:32.860
but that intersection between political

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.840
and social history on the one hand

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:39.050
and artistry and cultural production

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:40.930
and genre on the other.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:43.570
I mean, that's kind of where I
live as a cultural historian.

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:46.150
So it just felt like a
really rich opportunity

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:50.300
and her archive is really
quite extraordinary

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:51.873
for that kind of reflection.

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:55.220
- [Stephen] Let me ask
you a particular question

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:57.163
about her relationship to religion,

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.237
which as you said, is a primary focus

00:19:01.237 --> 00:19:03.320
of one of your four sections.

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:07.080
So you quote Odetta
registering some distrust

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.420
of institutional religion,
or at least the leaders

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:14.420
of institutional churches
in the postwar period.

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:16.877
She would later recall that quote,

00:19:16.877 --> 00:19:19.720
"I didn't believe anything
the minister said.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:22.103
When the music started I trusted that."

00:19:23.049 --> 00:19:24.860
Could you talk a little bit more about.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:29.710
- Yeah, this was not an
easy piece of writing for me

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:30.760
for several reasons.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:34.590
One, I'm not Christian

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:40.010
and I'm not an African American Christian.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:44.120
So, there's some sensitivity

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.720
about how are you gonna
approach materials like this?

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:49.980
So there's that, but there's also,

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:52.800
I think the historian's tendency

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:57.120
and I think this runs pretty
deep in civil rights history

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:01.160
as a genre, to take too literally

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:06.160
some of the liberationist's
strains of Christian theology

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:10.260
and to kind of map them onto
the politics of the time,

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:13.140
and clearly that was important.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.620
It's important that the black church

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:19.780
was such a crucial site for the struggle

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:22.310
in the 1940s and '50s.

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:24.540
And that's where a figure

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:27.610
like Martin Luther King emerges from.

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:30.670
It's crucial that King
himself is citing chapter

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:33.960
and verse all the time
in his political oratory.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.250
So these things are important,

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:38.373
but I think they're too
easily oversimplified.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:42.390
This particular chapter was
something that I struggled with

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.370
because I think I
oversimplified it myself.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:48.630
I'd really just tried to read
African American Christianity

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.500
as liberation theology.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:55.500
And to kind of too facilely
read the political symbolism

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:03.790
of the ways that a figure like
Odetta was using the gospels.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:10.430
And I was fortunate to
be corrected in that

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:14.170
by our colleague, Katie
Lofton in religious studies,

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:16.110
who read an early draft of the chapter

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:20.070
and said, no, no, no, no,
you have to understand

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:23.396
that Christianity is a problem too.

00:21:23.396 --> 00:21:25.310
It's not just the answer to the problem,

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.420
it's also part of the problem.

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:29.410
And she gave me some reading assignments,

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:33.060
including a fantastic book
by another of our colleagues,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:36.683
Willie Jennings on racism
and the Christian tradition,

00:21:37.905 --> 00:21:38.738
along with many other things.

00:21:38.738 --> 00:21:41.800
And it was actually through the rethinking

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:45.240
that Katie Lofton had urged,

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:49.170
that I actually found
some of those quotes,

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:50.900
including the one that you just mentioned,

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.410
from Odetta herself,
talking about religion

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:58.410
in a much more problematized
way, and in a richer way.

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:01.450
So one of the things I
tried to do in that chapter

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:05.450
is not just lose the liberationists strand

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:10.450
of Christian theology
and Christian symbology,

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.700
but also to try to capture
some of the complexities

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:16.920
and even the paradoxes
that are embedded in it

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:21.920
in the ways that if it is true,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:27.060
that Christianity was
one of the symbol systems

00:22:27.160 --> 00:22:31.070
for imagining liberation
on the part of the slaves.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.570
It was also one of the symbol systems

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:36.370
that allowed for slavery
in the first place.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:38.410
And so to try to really
think about that paradox

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:40.270
and to think about how
that carries through

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:45.020
over the 100 years leading up
to 1963 when Odetta is singing

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:47.220
and how she's kind of positioning herself

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:50.450
in relation to those paradoxes

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:52.403
and those kinds of contending streams.

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:57.480
- [Stephen] So the book
in part positions Odetta

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:02.060
in dialogue with,
contention with in some ways

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.763
Christian theology, Christian
churches, church goers,

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:08.020
all developing at a time

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.940
in which the civil rights
movement is changing.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:13.600
You also think about Odetta,
as you mentioned already,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:16.320
as a figure shaped by the great migration.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:19.020
And I'd love to hear you
talk more about that.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:22.837
You say that at least a
few of her songs, quote,

00:23:22.837 --> 00:23:26.730
"Belong to the vast cross genres cannon

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.340
of post-migration black narratives,

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:31.010
alongside Jacob Lawrence's
migration series,

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:34.170
Richard Wright's 12 million Black Voices,

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:35.750
Langston Hughes's One-way Ticket,

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:38.530
or Bessie Smith's Gulf Coast Views.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.700
Can you say more about how her work,

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:43.330
produced outside of the
South for the most part,

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:45.530
engaged historical and
contemporary conditions

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:47.620
in that region and
understandings of the South?

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:52.200
- So the two songs that I
really focus on in this context

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:57.200
are Cotton Fields and Boll Weevil,

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.470
both of which are about the South.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:02.430
But the argument I try to make is that

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:07.430
they're very much, they're
performed outside of the South

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:11.900
and they're performed for the most part

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:15.430
for people who have removed
themselves from the South.

00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:17.570
But that that dimension
is really important.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:21.133
So they're a kind of
backward reflection on,

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:26.230
not just a kind of geographical space,

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:28.680
but a kind of historical space as well.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:32.340
So there's a temporal and
spatial geography to those songs

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.630
that I think is really important.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:39.630
And so it's not as literally
the kind of migration narrative

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:48.137
that so many other writers,
including singers, made famous.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:53.310
But I think it's in a similar
genre in the sense that

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:58.250
these are songs that could not
have been performed this way,

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:02.150
nor been thought of as
important in this way,

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:04.540
if they weren't on the other side

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:06.100
of the experience of migration.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.480
And if they weren't, in some ways,

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:10.820
a reflection back onto a space

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.720
that had been successfully escaped.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:17.150
I mean that I guess is the
thing that really defines them.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:19.170
And I think it's very subtle.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:21.570
One of the things I argue is that,

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.590
that if you listen really closely

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:27.230
and especially in Cotton Fields,

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:32.230
I think it's most subtle and
maybe it takes some study

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.610
to really hear it, but
there's a kind of agitation

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:39.680
in her version of that song
that really speaks about

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:43.530
the danger and the hazards

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.920
and the violence of the South

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:50.440
and what it might mean to escape it

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:53.810
or what it might mean, in the
literal context of lyrics,

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:55.253
to be entrapped within it.

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.890
- [Stephen] So you were
just to think about Odetta,

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:02.280
alongside many other cultural figures

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.390
in the mid and late 20th century.

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:07.380
And I just wanna throw
out some names that appear

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:09.180
in your book, "One Grain of Sand,"

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:10.970
and just ask you to say something

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:12.100
about one or more of them,

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:15.550
about their relationship
to Odetta and her work.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:19.700
I'm thinking of people like
Paul Robeson, Josh White,

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:23.400
Woody Guthrie, Pete Seeger, Nina Simone,

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:25.710
Bernice Johnson Reagon, and others.

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:29.230
- The name that jumps
out at me is Robeson,

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:34.230
because I think that his approach to art

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:39.800
was very much the approach
that Odetta herself adopted.

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:43.730
And whether she got it directly from him,

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:47.690
or indirectly through many
of the mutual acquaintances

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:50.990
that they had, I'm not sure.

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:55.990
But, his idea that that
artistic expression

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.470
could be political expression
was absolutely important

00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:06.410
to how she thought about
her task as a singer.

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:11.410
And also, I mean, the way
that I think as a stylist,

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:12.733
if that's the right word.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.340
Robeson was famous for performing

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:20.450
in this kind of operatic mode

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:23.720
that disrupted the expectations,

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:26.090
especially of white audiences.

00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:29.560
But it kind of busted some of the genres

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:32.130
of African American
performance at the time

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:33.780
in a way that I think was really important

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.210
and really powerful.

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:40.210
And I think that that is something
that Odetta took as well,

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:45.210
because as careful as
an archivist as she was,

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:50.640
in studying these old
African American standards

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:53.370
in the Smithsonian
collections and the like,

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.040
she was never simply trying to reproduce

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:57.020
what she was hearing.

00:27:57.020 --> 00:27:59.400
She actually was making the songs her own

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:00.940
in a really important way.

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:03.362
And the way I ended up putting it is that

00:28:03.362 --> 00:28:07.560
she inhabits the moral center of a song

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:11.500
more than just reproducing the textures

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:13.890
of the archive as she found it.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.710
And I think that that's a similar way,

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:17.220
although his style was
completely different.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:19.540
I think that Robeson did
something very similar.

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:23.913
When he sang a song with that
just booming operatic voice,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:29.690
he was doing something that
the archive was not giving him,

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:32.220
but he was bringing his own
special kind of power to it.

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.180
And I think that that's something

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.883
that she had in common with him.

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:40.910
- [Stephen] So as an
interpreter of the songs

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:43.490
that you write about, some
of which, many of which

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:45.630
were already in the folk repertoire,

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:48.350
you describe how Odetta also

00:28:49.823 --> 00:28:53.490
queered the gendered conventions of music

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:55.410
as it was inherited by her.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.120
Can you talk a little bit
about the ways in which

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:58.620
she queered those conventions?

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:04.690
- Yeah, I think it's an
important part of her work.

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:07.060
I mean, some of the things that she did,

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:10.090
well, by choosing the repertoire itself,

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:14.230
I mean, most of the
songs that she performed

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:18.563
came out of a very male kind of tradition.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:23.020
And some of the things
that she did with her voice

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:28.070
played with a kind of gender neutrality

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:30.370
or ambiguity that I think
was really important

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:32.920
to the power of the song.

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:35.180
And it was something that a
lot of critics at the time

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:38.110
commented on without knowing
exactly how to understand it.

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:41.460
A lot of white listeners
didn't like it, frankly.

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.510
They wanted her to sound more like a lady,

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:44.690
or appear more like a lady.

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:48.350
But I think that her conscious decision

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:52.180
not to be that performer
was part of how she saw

00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:54.220
the politics of what she was doing.

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:59.180
I mean, it was about kind of
disrupting the status quo,

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.210
disrupting expectation, pushing
people to think in new ways.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.780
And I think that for her gender was

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:12.780
as important a part of
that project as race was.

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:16.450
- [Stephen] Now I wanted to invite you

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:20.010
to talk a little bit more
about Odetta as a performer.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:21.490
You've written of course, extensively

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:26.490
about this album as a piece,
as an archive in and of itself.

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:31.163
But you also write in the
book about Odetta on stage,

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:33.480
what she did for audiences,

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:36.130
how audiences understood
her in the various settings

00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:40.320
in which she performed,
before and after 1963.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:41.650
So I wonder if you could take us through

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:44.710
some sense of what
audiences would have seen,

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:45.780
what they would have appreciated,

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:47.130
what they would have heard,

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:51.340
had they been in one of
Odetta's performances

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:54.307
around the time in which
"One Grain of Sand" appeared?

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:01.310
- It's important to
remember how young she was.

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:04.710
She was still just in her late 20s.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:07.173
And so she was still
developing as an artist,

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:10.330
or maybe early thirties by then.

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:13.070
But she was still developing as an artist

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:13.930
and she was changing.

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:17.400
And now with the advantage of retrospect,

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:18.710
you can really see that.

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:21.427
One of the most striking things about her

00:31:21.427 --> 00:31:22.950
and this was part of her power

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:24.840
and it was something that
she never stopped using,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.360
was that classical training.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:31.360
I mean, she held the stage
with the kind of majesty

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.310
of the great operatic singers.

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:36.333
She just did.

00:31:37.923 --> 00:31:41.860
She held the stage in a way
that she just looked immovable.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.013
She looked as immovable as she sounded.

00:31:45.183 --> 00:31:47.840
It was part of the power
of her performance.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:50.440
In those years, she had
a kind of seriousness

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:55.260
that I think was more
characteristic of her performances

00:31:55.260 --> 00:31:56.760
in those years than later on.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:00.650
She, her friends would say,
was one of the funniest people

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:02.100
you would ever meet in your life.

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:04.130
And there's plenty of evidence for that.

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:07.480
And you can hear that in some
of the later live recordings.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.520
But for the most part in the later ones,

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:13.060
you don't hear that so much in
the late '50s or early '60s.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.260
I think that became more a part

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:18.863
of her kind of stage patter later on.

00:32:21.443 --> 00:32:23.720
She had a fearsome sense of humor

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:27.043
and she over time learned
how to deploy that.

00:32:28.055 --> 00:32:30.250
But I think that that was
less a part of her act

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:32.820
in the years that we're talking about.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:34.993
I think the more striking thing,

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:39.360
through probably the late '60s

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:43.097
was just the power of her presence

00:32:43.097 --> 00:32:45.053
and her seriousness of purpose.

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:49.350
- [Stephen] So Matt you've
written about Odetta.

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:51.180
You've spoken to many people about Odetta.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:52.320
You've taught about Odetta.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.804
I know she's made her way into the courses

00:32:54.804 --> 00:32:57.180
that you've given over the years.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:59.880
And my sense is from hearing you talk

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:01.630
that you think of her as a person

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:04.960
who was profoundly influential
in the late 20th century,

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:08.570
but also perhaps a person
who's not widely recognized

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:09.640
by many people today.

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:11.960
So can you talk a little bit more about

00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:16.960
how you see the influence of
Odetta on say other musicians?

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:20.560
You mention this in the
book, people like Joan Baez,

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:22.940
Rhiannon Giddens, her influence on

00:33:22.940 --> 00:33:24.570
politically-engaged Americans,

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:28.180
people like Rosa Parks,
artists like Spike Lee,

00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:29.480
perhaps the ways we understand

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:32.440
African American history today.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.260
Love to hear you talk a
little bit more about her

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:36.520
perhaps unrecognized influence.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:37.650
- Yeah, it's interesting.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:39.110
And it's one of the puzzles really

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:41.740
that I never quite resolved is,

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:44.810
why is it that our students
really don't know her?

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:46.670
I mean, she's almost unknown

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:49.080
among younger Americans right now.

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:51.340
Although I think that
that may be changing.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.000
My book, it turns out,
is the first of a flurry

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:56.200
that are gonna be coming
out soon about her.

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:59.920
So maybe that will change.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:04.310
But it is really fascinating
that after about the '70s,

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:05.920
or maybe the '80s, when she was still,

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:08.570
she was on the college circuit,
she would travel around,

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:09.920
she would play at college campuses,

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:13.380
and she was still well known
among college students,

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:15.250
certainly when I was
in college in the '70s

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:17.163
and probably beyond that.

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:20.490
But then really kind of drops out

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:25.490
as someone who is kind of
famous to a younger generation.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:32.060
Which is just kind of
odd because she was truly

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:33.610
one of the most famous performers

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:35.090
of her generation at the time.

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:38.210
I mean, at the time that she's standing

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:41.922
next to Martin Luther King in 1963,

00:34:41.922 --> 00:34:43.500
everybody knew who she was

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.350
and everybody knew why she was there.

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:48.250
So it's kind of startling.

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:51.820
But that ceased to be
the case at some point.

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:54.610
On the other hand, she never stopped being

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:56.250
a musician's musician.

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:58.080
And I think she still is very much that.

00:34:58.080 --> 00:35:01.670
So, you mention her name to
someone like Rhiannon Giddens,

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:05.480
and there's an immediate enthusiasm

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:09.260
and excitement to talk about who she was

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:11.010
and her influence and what she did.

00:35:13.418 --> 00:35:16.550
I've been privileged to
have this conversation

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:21.550
with several current singers,
like Giddens or Valerie June.

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:27.810
And for them, Odetta never went away.

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:29.816
I mean, she never stopped being important.

00:35:29.816 --> 00:35:34.816
It's one of the important
and puzzling perhaps

00:35:36.630 --> 00:35:40.923
things about the arc of
her career and her fame.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:46.550
Bob Dylan famously said, that he picked up

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:50.440
a flat-top Gibson guitar
because of Odetta.

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:53.333
That was the power of her
influence at the time.

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:59.670
And somehow that just faded for listeners.

00:36:03.986 --> 00:36:06.520
And I guess folk as a genre
is not nearly as popular now

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:08.500
as it was a generation ago.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:12.310
But still, people, our students know

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:13.743
who Woody Guthrie was.

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:19.570
But rare is the student
who knows who Odetta was

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:20.470
until I tell them.

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:23.367
- [Stephen] Well, I would
just say the book, you know,

00:36:23.367 --> 00:36:25.140
"One Grain of Sand" goes a long way

00:36:25.140 --> 00:36:28.430
towards explaining Odetta I
think, for audiences today

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:32.760
and contextualizing her and
using her to help rethink

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.520
the ways in which African American history

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.950
from roughly 1863 to roughly 1963,

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:43.950
with bleeding on both
sides, was formulated by her

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:47.382
and can be reinterpreted with her in mind,

00:36:47.382 --> 00:36:51.080
by historians, artists, and others today.

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:53.540
And I guess I just wanna
ask you by way of closing,

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:56.720
if you have any other
thoughts about the ways

00:36:56.720 --> 00:36:59.800
in which you hope that the
book that you've written

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:01.550
will work in the world.

00:37:01.550 --> 00:37:03.940
What are some of the things
that you would love readers,

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:07.740
whether they're students, or
fellow academics, or others

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:12.050
to appreciate about
Odetta or to be thinking

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:14.250
as they walk away from this book,

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:16.410
about other projects
that they might take up,

00:37:16.410 --> 00:37:18.460
or other ways of understanding the world?

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:21.443
- I really appreciate the question.

00:37:22.610 --> 00:37:25.510
Even if I've learned over the years

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:29.163
to try to be very modest
in my ambitions for a book,

00:37:30.241 --> 00:37:32.190
'cause when you send
it out into the world,

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:34.440
you just never know what
people are gonna do with it,

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.500
or how it will be taken up.

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:38.450
I guess my fondest hopes would be twofold.

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:42.310
One would be that it would urge people

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:45.210
to pick up her records
again and listen to them

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:50.210
and really think about the
brilliance of her artistry

00:37:50.530 --> 00:37:53.610
and the power of the political work

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:55.400
that she did through her singing.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:59.810
I mean, if in some small
way, the book contributed

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:03.210
to a renewed interest in Odetta herself,

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:04.800
that would be probably the most

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.973
a person could hope for writing a book.

00:38:09.170 --> 00:38:10.820
The other thing I would say though is that

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:12.530
there is something about method

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:15.450
that's embedded in the book
that I hope our students

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:16.740
would pick up as well.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:19.700
That intersection that I mentioned before,

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:24.700
that zone where social and
political history meet culture,

00:38:26.630 --> 00:38:29.090
and the culture industries
and cultural production

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:33.730
and artistry and genre, is
a really, really rich zone.

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:37.430
And I would hope that
book might model something

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:41.070
about cultural analysis
and the ways in which

00:38:42.110 --> 00:38:47.080
something as seemingly
self-evident as a song

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:49.490
can actually open up all kinds of doors

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:50.713
for historical inquiry.

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:55.120
So that's maybe the wider
ambition of the book.

00:38:55.120 --> 00:39:00.120
But if the book achieves
anything at all, I'll be happy.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:04.900
- [Stephen] I think it achieves a lot.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:07.210
Matthew Frye Jacobson
thank you for talking to us

00:39:07.210 --> 00:39:10.370
about your most recent
book "One Grain of Sand."

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:11.680
- Thank you so much for the invitation.

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:13.230
This has really been a delight.

